helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_queries.xhp	0	help	par_idN105F9				0	cy	\<variable id=\"data_queries\"\>\<link href=\"text/shared/guide/data_queries.xhp\"\>Working with Queries\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_queries.xhp	0	help	par_idN10617				0	cy	If you often want to access only a subset of your data that can be well defined by a filter condition, you can define a query. This is basically a name for the new view at the filtered data. You open the query and see the current data in the table layout that you defined.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_queries.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061A				0	cy	Creating a New Query With the Query Wizard				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_queries.xhp	0	help	par_idN1061E				0	cy	In %PRODUCTNAME you can create a new query using the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/querywizard00.xhp\"\>Query Wizard\</link\>:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_queries.xhp	0	help	par_idN10632				0	cy	Open the database file where you want to create the new query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_queries.xhp	0	help	par_idN10636				0	cy	In the left pane of the database window, click the \<emph\>Queries\</emph\> icon.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\guide\data_queries.xhp	0	help	par_idN1063E				0	cy	Click \<emph\>Use Wizard to Create Query\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
