helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159176				0	cy	Some databases also support the creation of a new table view. Selecting the \<emph\>Create View\</emph\> command from the \<emph\>Tables\</emph\> tab page of a database document, you see the \<emph\>View Design\</emph\> window that resembles the \<emph\>Query Design\</emph\> window described here.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149233				0	cy	The Design View				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145673				0	cy	To create a query, click the \<emph\>Queries\</emph\> icon in a database document, then click \<emph\>Create Query in Design View\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152474				0	cy	In the top of the query Design View window, the \<link href=\"text/shared/main0214.xhp\" name=\"icons\"\>icons\</link\> of the \<emph\>Query Design\</emph\> Bar and the \<emph\>Design\</emph\> bar are displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147559				0	cy	If you want to test a query, double-click the query name in the database document. The query result is displayed in a table similar to the Data Source View. Note: the table displayed is only temporary.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8554338				0	cy	Preview				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154939				0	cy	Browse				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148799				0	cy	When you open the query design for the first time, in order to create a new query, you can click \<link href=\"text/shared/02/14020100.xhp\" name=\"Add Tables\"\>\<emph\>Add Tables\</emph\>\</link\>. You then see a dialog in which you must first select the table that will be the basis for the query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144762				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_CTL_QRYDGNTAB\"\>Double-click fields to add them to the query. Drag-and-drop to define relations.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157894				0	cy	While designing a query, you cannot modify the selected tables.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149562				0	cy	Remove tables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150685				0	cy	To remove the table from Design View, click the upper border of the table window and display the context menu. You can use the \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> command to remove the table from the Design View. Another option is to press the Delete key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150012				0	cy	Move table and modify table size				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146922				0	cy	You can resize and arrange the tables according to your preferences. To move tables, drag the upper border to the desired position. Enlarge or reduce the size in which the table is displayed by positioning the mouse cursor on a border or on a corner and dragging the table until it is the desired size.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145365				0	cy	Table Relations				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154145				0	cy	If there are data relations between a field name in one table and a field name in another table, you can use these relations for your query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152577				0	cy	If, for example, you have a spreadsheet for articles identified by an article number, and a spreadsheet for customers in which you record all articles that a customer orders using the corresponding article numbers, then there is a relationship between the two "article number" data fields. If you now want to create a query that returns all articles that a customer has ordered, you must retrieve data from two spreadsheets. To do this, you must tell $[officename] what the relationship exists between the data in the two spreadsheets.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155302				0	cy	To do this, click a field name in a table (for example, the field name "Item-Number" from the Customer table), hold down the mouse button and then drag the field name to the field name of the other table ("Item-Number" from the Item table). When you release the mouse button, a line connecting the two fields in the two windows appears. The corresponding condition that the content of the two field names must be identical is entered in the resulting SQL query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153876				0	cy	The creation of a query that is based on several related sheets is only possible if you use $[officename] as the interface for a relational database.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145646				0	cy	You cannot access tables from different databases in a query. Queries involving multiple tables can only be created within one database.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153279				0	cy	Specifying link type				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154791				0	cy	If you double-click the line connecting two linked fields or call the menu command \<emph\>Insert - New Relation\</emph\>, you can specify the type of link in the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010101.xhp\" name=\"Relations\"\>\<emph\>Relations\</emph\>\</link\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150094	285			0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QUERY_EDIT_JOINCONNECTION\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Edit Joint Properties.\</ahelp\>Alternatively, press Tab until the joint vector is selected (it is displayed enlarged), then press Shift+F10 to display the context menu and there choose the command \<emph\>Edit\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155851				0	cy	Deleting relations				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156178				0	cy	To delete a relation between two tables, click the connection line and then press the Delete key.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715	284			0	cy	Alternatively, delete the respective entries in \<emph\>Fields involved \</emph\>in the \<emph\>Relations\</emph\> dialog. Or press Tab until the connecting vector is displayed highlighted, then press Shift+F10 to open the context menu and select \<emph\>Delete \</emph\>command.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151208				0	cy	Define query				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3158416				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_CTL_QRYDGNCRIT\"\>Select conditions to define the query.\</ahelp\> Each column of the design table accepts a data field for the query. The conditions in one row are linked with a Boolean AND.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154161				0	cy	Specify field name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146791				0	cy	First, select all field names from the tables that you want to add to the query. You can do this either with drag-and-drop or by double-clicking a field name in the table window. With the drag-and-drop method, use the mouse to drag a field name from the table window into the lower area of the query design. As you do this, you can decide which column you want to add the field to. Select a field name by double-clicking. It will then be added to the next free column.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150750				0	cy	Deleting field names				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154479				0	cy	To remove a field name from the query, click the column header of the field and choose the \<emph\>Delete\</emph\> command on the context menu for the column.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155764				0	cy	Save query				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148481				0	cy	Use the \<emph\>Save\</emph\> icon on the Standard Bar to save the query. You see a dialog that asks you to enter a name for the query. If the database supports schemas, you can also enter a schema.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154362				0	cy	Schema				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"DBACCESS_COMBOBOX_DLG_SAVE_AS_ET_SCHEMA\"\>Enter the name of the schema that is assigned to the query or table view.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156717				0	cy	Query name or table view name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154253				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"DBACCESS_EDIT_DLG_SAVE_AS_ET_TITLE\"\>Enter the name of the query or table view.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163805				0	cy	Filtering data				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154964				0	cy	To filter data for the query, set the desired preferences in the lower area of the Design View. The following lines are available:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146916				0	cy	Field				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156372				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FIELD\"\>Enter the name of the data field that you referred to in the Query. All settings made in the lower rows refer to this field.\</ahelp\> If you activate a cell with a mouse click you'll see an arrow button, which enables you to select a field. The "Table name.*" option selects all data fields and the criteria is valid for all table fields.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145150				0	cy	Alias				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146315				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_ALIAS\"\>Specifies an alias. This alias will be listed in a query instead of the field name. This makes it possible to use user-defined column labels.\</ahelp\> For example, if the data field has the name PtNo and, instead of that name, you would like to have PartNum appear in the query, enter PartNum as alias.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155959				0	cy	In an SQL statement, aliases are defined as following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149922				0	cy	SELECT column AS alias FROM table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159335				0	cy	For example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148478				0	cy	SELECT "PtNo" AS "PartNum" FROM "Parts"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148485				0	cy	Table				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3163665				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_TABLE\"\>The corresponding database table of the selected data field is listed here.\</ahelp\> If you activate the a cell with a mouse click, an arrow will appear which enables you to select another table of the current query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154207				0	cy	Sort				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150979				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_ORDER\"\>If you click the cell, you can select among the sorting options: ascending, descending and not sorted.\</ahelp\> Text fields will be sorted alphabetically (A to Z) and numerical fields numerically (0 to 9).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150384				0	cy	Visible				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146133				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_VISIBLE\"\>If you mark the \<emph\>Visible\</emph\> property for a data field, that field will be visible in the query\</ahelp\>. If you only use a data field to formulate a condition, you do not necessarily need to show it.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154714				0	cy	Criteria				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145134				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_CRIT\"\>Specifies the \<link href=\"text/shared/explorer/database/02010100.xhp\" name=\"criteria \"\>criteria \</link\>by which the content of the data field should be filtered.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152477				0	cy	or				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154585				0	cy	Here you can enter one additional criterion for filtering in each line. Multiple criteria in one column will be connected by an OR link.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148800				0	cy	You can also use the context menu of the line headers in the lower area of the query design to insert another line for functions:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148419				0	cy	Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153233	152			0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\" visibility=\"hidden\"\>Select a function to run in the query here.\</ahelp\> The functions you can run here depend on the database. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id8760818				0	cy	If you are working with the Adabas database, the list box in the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> row offers you the following options:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150307				0	cy	Option				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166430				0	cy	SQL				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152993				0	cy	Effect				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155377				0	cy	No function				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155533				0	cy	No function will be executed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3166420				0	cy	Average				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145268				0	cy	AVG				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154486				0	cy	Calculates the arithmetic mean of a field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149979				0	cy	Count				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154260				0	cy	COUNT				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155810				0	cy	Determines the number of records in the table. Empty fields can either be counted (a) or not (b).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151333				0	cy	a) COUNT(*): Passing an asterisk as the argument counts all records in the table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152889				0	cy	b) COUNT(column): Passing a field name as an argument counts only fields in which the field name in question contains a value. Null values (empty fields) will not be counted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153067				0	cy	Maximum				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148840				0	cy	MAX				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159221				0	cy	Determines the highest value of a field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146866				0	cy	Minimum				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148604				0	cy	MIN				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3157982				0	cy	Determines the lowest value of a field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154828				0	cy	Sum				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147070				0	cy	SUM				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154536				0	cy	Calculates the sum of values of associated fields.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148820				0	cy	Group				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145375				0	cy	GROUP BY				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149438				0	cy	Groups query data according to the field name selected. Functions are executed according to the specified groups. In SQL, this option corresponds to the GROUP BY clause. If a criterion is added, this entry appears in the SQL HAVING.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156038				0	cy	You can also enter function calls directly into the SQL statement. The syntax is:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3156340				0	cy	SELECT FUNCTION(column) FROM table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155075				0	cy	For example, the function call in SQL for calculating a sum is:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154591				0	cy	SELECT SUM("Price") FROM "Article".				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3159205				0	cy	Except for the \<emph\>Group\</emph\> function, the above functions are so-called Aggregate functions. These are functions that calculate data to create summaries from the results. Additional functions that are not listed in the list box are also possible. These depend on the specific database system in use. To get information about driver specific functions refer to the documentation of your database system.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148651				0	cy	To use other functions not listed in the list box, you must enter them under \<emph\>Field\</emph\>. They will then appear automatically in the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155098				0	cy	You can also assign aliases to function calls. If the query is not to be displayed in the column header, enter the desired name under \<emph\>Alias\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155539				0	cy	The corresponding function in an SQL statement is:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149425				0	cy	SELECT FUNCTION() AS alias FROM table				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3144431				0	cy	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154614				0	cy	SELECT COUNT(*) AS count FROM "Item"				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154610				0	cy	If you run this function, you cannot insert any additional columns for the query other than receiving these columns as a "Group" function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154644				0	cy	\<emph\>Examples\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151120				0	cy	In the following example, a query is run through two tables: an "Item" table with the "Item_No" field and a "Suppliers" table with the "Supplier_Name" field. In addition, both tables have a common field name "Supplier_No."				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155144				0	cy	The following steps are required to create a query containing all suppliers who deliver more than three items.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153240				0	cy	Insert the "Item" and "Suppliers" tables into the query design.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148807				0	cy	Link the "Supplier_No" fields of the two tables if there is not already a relation of this type.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3161652				0	cy	Double-click the "Item_No" field from the "Item" table. Display the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line using the context menu and select the Count function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151009				0	cy	Enter >3 as a criterion and disable the Visible field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3145601				0	cy	Double-click the "Supplier_Name" field in the "Suppliers" table and choose the Group function.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147512				0	cy	Run the query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3148638				0	cy	If the "price" (for the individual price of an article) and "Supplier_No" (for the supplier of the article) fields exist in the "Item" table, you can obtain the average price of the item that a supplier provides with the following query:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153045				0	cy	Insert the "Item" table into the query design.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149802				0	cy	Double-click the "Price" and "Supplier_No" fields.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153554				0	cy	Enable the \<emph\>Function\</emph\> line and select the Average function from the "Price" field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155597				0	cy	You can also enter "Average" in the line for the alias name (without quotation marks).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151191				0	cy	Choose Group for the "Supplier_No" field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155547				0	cy	Run the query.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147549				0	cy	The following context menu commands and symbols are available:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154172				0	cy	Functions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150414				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides a row for selection of functions.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149872				0	cy	Table Name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147246				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the table name.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145117				0	cy	Alias Name				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155754				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Shows or hides the row for the alias name.\</ahelp\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153298				0	cy	Distinct Values				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3147500				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_QRYDGN_ROW_FUNCTION\"\>Applies only distinct values to the query.\</ahelp\> This applies to records containing data that appears several times in the selected fields. If the \<emph\>Distinct Values\</emph\> command is active, you will see only one record in the query (DISTINCT). Otherwise, you will see all records corresponding to the query criteria (ALL).				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150436	159			0	cy	For example, if the name "Smith" occurs several times in your address database, you can choose the\<emph\> Distinct Values\</emph\> command to specify in the query that the name "Smith" will occur only once.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3152352	160			0	cy	For a query involving several fields, the combination of values from all fields must be unique so that the result can be formed from a specific record. For example, you have "Smith in Chicago" once in your address book and "Smith in London" twice. With the\<emph\> Distinct Values\</emph\> command, the query will use the two fields "last name" and "city" and return the query result "Smith in Chicago" once and "Smith in London" once.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3149825				0	cy	In SQL, this command corresponds to the DISTINCT predicate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148926				0	cy	Formulating filter conditions				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\02010100.xhp	0	help	par_id3153162				0	cy	When formulating filter conditions, various operators and commands are available to you. Apart from the relational operators, there are SQL-specific commands that query the content of database fields. If you use these commands in the $[officename] syntax, $[officename] automatically converts these into the corresponding SQL syntax. You can also enter the SQL command directly. The following tables give an overview of the operators and commands:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
