helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3151054				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_AUTORETRIEVEENABLED\"\>Enables $[officename] support of auto-incremented data fields for the current ODBC or JDBC data source.\</ahelp\> Select this check box if the database does not support the auto-increment feature in its SDBCX layer. In general, the auto-increment is selected for the primary key field.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150400				0	cy	Auto-increment statement				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154366	27			0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_AUTOINCREMENTVALUE\"\>Enter the SQL command specifier that instructs the data source to auto-increment a specified Integer data field.\</ahelp\> For example, a typical SQL statement to create a data field is: 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3159149				0	cy	CREATE TABLE "table1" ("id" INTEGER)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3147084				0	cy	To auto-increment the "id" data field in a MySQL database, change the statement to:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3154909				0	cy	CREATE TABLE "table1" ("id" INTEGER AUTO_INCREMENT)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3152933				0	cy	In other words, enter AUTO_INCREMENT into \<emph\>Auto-increment statement\</emph\> box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149765				0	cy	Query of generated values				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145171				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_RETRIEVE_AUTO\"\>Enter an SQL statement that returns the last auto-incremented value for the primary key data field.\</ahelp\> For example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3150769				0	cy	SELECT LAST_INSERT_D();				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3157892				0	cy	Use SQL92 naming constraints				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\shared\explorer\database\11020000.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	cy	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_DSADMIN_SQL92CHECK\"\>Only allows names that use characters that conform to the SQL92 naming constraints in the data source. All other characters are rejected.\</ahelp\> Each name must begin with a lower or upper case letter, or an underline ( _ ). The remaining characters can be ASCII letters, underlines, and numbers.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
