helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149377				0	cy	\<bookmark_value\>lines; drawing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>curves; drawing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>control points;definition\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>corner points\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>drawing;lines\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	cy	\<variable id=\"line_draw\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/line_draw.xhp\" name=\"Drawing Curves\"\>Drawing Curves\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3148868	4			0	cy	The \<emph\>Curve\</emph\> tool \<image id=\"img_id3150205\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_linetoolbox.png\" width=\"5.64mm\" height=\"5.64mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150205\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\> lets you draw a curved line that consists of one or more line segments. Each Bézier curve segment is defined by two data points (endpoints) and one or two control points (handles) that you can drag to adjust the arc of the curve. A control point is connected to a data point by a control line. Moving a control point pulls the curve towards it, and changes the shape of a curve.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210				0	cy	A Bézier curve has three types of data points:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3154766				0	cy	A symmetrical data point has the same line curvature on either side, and two control lines that move together as a straight line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3149874				0	cy	A smooth data point has different line curvatures on either side, and two control lines that move together as a straight line.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3150435				0	cy	A corner data point has one or two control lines and joins different line segments.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3155928	6			0	cy	Click where you want the curve to start, and drag a short distance in the direction you want the curve to go. To create a straight line segment, hold down \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Shift\</item\> while you drag.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3148390				0	cy	Release the mouse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3154865	22			0	cy	Move the pointer to where you want the curve segment to end and do one of the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id1556443				0	cy	Do one of the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3151172	9			0	cy	Double-click to finish drawing the line. To create a closed shape, double-click the starting point of the line. 				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3153919				0	cy	Click to draw the curve segment using the current arc.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_draw.xhp	0	help	par_id3149451				0	cy	Drag in any direction to define an arc for the curve, and then click to set it.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
