helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	tit				0	cy	Duplicating Objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145750				0	cy	\<bookmark_value\>doubling draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>draw objects; duplicating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>duplicating draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>multiplying draw objects\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145750				0	cy	\<variable id=\"duplicate_object\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/duplicate_object.xhp\" name=\"Duplicating Objects\"\>Duplicating Objects\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3149400				0	cy	You can create duplicate or multiple copies of an object. The copies can be identical or can differ in size, color, orientation and location.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	cy	The following example creates a stack of coins by making multiple copies of a single ellipse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3149129				0	cy	Use the \<emph\>Ellipse\</emph\> tool to draw a solid yellow ellipse.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3149209				0	cy	Select the ellipse and choose \<emph\>Edit - Duplicate\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3145585	9			0	cy	Enter 12 as \<emph\>Number of copies.\</emph\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3151192				0	cy	Enter a negative value for the \<emph\>Width\</emph\> and \<emph\>Height\</emph\> so that the coins decrease in size as you go up the stack.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3151387				0	cy	To define a color transition for the coins, select different colors in the \<emph\>Start\</emph\> and \<emph\>End\</emph\> boxes. The \<emph\>Start\</emph\> color is applied to the object that you are duplicating.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3149947				0	cy	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\> to create the duplicates.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\duplicate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3153935				0	cy	\<link href=\"text/simpress/01/02120000.xhp\" name=\"Edit - Duplicate\"\>Edit - Duplicate\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	tit				0	cy	Cross-Fading Two Objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150715				0	cy	\<bookmark_value\>draw objects; cross-fading two objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>cross-fading; two draw objects\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150715				0	cy	\<variable id=\"cross_fading\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/cross_fading.xhp\" name=\"Cross-Fading Two Objects\"\>Cross-Fading Two Objects\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3154754				0	cy	Cross-fading creates shapes and distributes them by uniform increments between two drawing objects.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3155112				0	cy	The cross-fading command is only available in $[officename] Draw. You can, however, copy and paste cross-faded objects into $[officename] Impress.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149209				0	cy	To cross-fade two objects:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3150370				0	cy	Hold down Shift and click each object.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3166428				0	cy	Choose \<emph\>Edit - Cross-fading\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3156450				0	cy	Enter a value to specify the number of objects between the start and end of the cross-fade in the \<emph\>Increments\</emph\> box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3149405				0	cy	Click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3151240				0	cy	A group containing the two original objects and the specified number (increments) of cross-faded objects is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3159203				0	cy	\<image id=\"img_id3150210\" src=\"res/helpimg/ueberblenden.png\" width=\"74.88mm\" height=\"65.62mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3150210\"\>Illustration for crossfading\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\cross_fading.xhp	0	help	par_id3155760				0	cy	\<link href=\"text/simpress/01/02150000.xhp\" name=\"Editing - Cross-fading\"\>Editing - Cross-fading\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	tit				0	cy	Rotating Objects				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	bm_id3154684				0	cy	\<bookmark_value\>rotating; draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>draw objects; rotating\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>pivot points of draw objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>skewing draw objects\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154684				0	cy	\<variable id=\"rotate_object\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/rotate_object.xhp\" name=\"Rotating Objects\"\>Rotating Objects\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	cy	You can rotate an object around its default pivot point (center point) or a pivot point that you designate.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3146975				0	cy	\<image id=\"img_id3154729\" src=\"res/commandimagelist/sc_toggleobjectrotatemode.png\" width=\"5.64mm\" height=\"5.64mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3154729\"\>Icon\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3150716				0	cy	Select the object you want to rotate. On the \<emph\>Mode\</emph\> toolbar in $[officename] Draw or on the \<emph\>Drawing\</emph\> bar in $[officename] Impress, click the \<emph\>Rotate\</emph\> icon.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3149021				0	cy	Move the pointer to a corner handle so that the pointer changes to a rotate symbol. Drag the handle to rotate the object.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3155962				0	cy	\<image id=\"img_id3154023\" src=\"res/helpimg/rotieren.png\" width=\"54.24mm\" height=\"39.16mm\"\>\<alt id=\"alt_id3154023\"\>Rotate object\</alt\>\</image\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3166424				0	cy	To change the pivot point, drag the small circle in the center of the object to a new location.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\rotate_object.xhp	0	help	par_id3159236				0	cy	To skew the object vertically or horizontally, drag one of the side handles.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	tit				0	cy	Defining Custom Colors				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	bm_id3149263				0	cy	\<bookmark_value\>colors; defining and saving\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>user-defined colors\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>custom colors\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149263				0	cy	\<variable id=\"color_define\"\>\<link href=\"text/sdraw/guide/color_define.xhp\" name=\"Defining Custom Colors\"\>Defining Custom Colors\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154511				0	cy	If you want, you can mix a custom color and add it to a color table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155600				0	cy	To define a custom color				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3150327				0	cy	Choose \<emph\>Format - Area\</emph\> and click the \<emph\>Colors\</emph\> tab. A table of the predefined colors is displayed.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3154657				0	cy	Changes made to the standard color table are permanent and are saved automatically.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3166425				0	cy	Click a color in the table that is similar to the one you want to mix. The color appears in the upper preview box to the right of the table.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152992				0	cy	Select the RGB or CMYK color model in the box below the preview boxes.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152987	16			0	cy	The RGB color model mixes red, green and blue light to create colors on a computer screen. In the RGB model, the three color components are additive and can have values ranging from 0 (black) to 255 (white). The CMYK color model combines Cyan (C), Magenta (M), Yellow (Y), and blacK (K, also used for "Key") to create colors for printing. The four colors of the CMYK models are subtractive and are defined as percentages. Black corresponds to 100 % and white to 0 %.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145386				0	cy	Enter a numeric value in the boxes next to the color components. The new color appears in the preview box directly above the color model box.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3152871				0	cy	You can also create a color using a color spectrum. Click the \<emph\>Edit\</emph\> button to open the \<link href=\"text/shared/optionen/01010501.xhp\" name=\"Color\"\>\<emph\>Color\</emph\>\</link\> dialog. Click a color and click OK. Use the Hue, Saturation, and Brightness boxes to adjust your color selection.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3153011				0	cy	Do one of the following:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3147244				0	cy	If you want to replace the color in the standard color table that your custom color is based on, click \<emph\>Modify\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145116				0	cy	If you want to add your custom color to the standard color table, enter a name in the \<emph\>Name\</emph\> text box and click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sdraw\guide\color_define.xhp	0	help	par_id3145236				0	cy	\<link href=\"text/shared/01/03170000.xhp\" name=\"Color bar\"\>Color bar\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
