helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156384				0	cy	\<variable id=\"align\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/align.xhp\" name=\"Manually Aligning Formula Parts\"\>Manually Aligning Formula Parts\</link\>\</variable\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154657				0	cy	How do you align characters in $[officename] Math quickly and easily?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3150249				0	cy	To accomplish this, you must define empty groups and character strings. They do not require any space, but carry information that helps in the alignment process.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153912				0	cy	To create empty groups, enter curly brackets \<emph\>{}\</emph\> in the Commands window. In the following example, the goal is to achieve a line break so that the plus signs are beneath one another, even though one less character is entered in the upper line:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153246				0	cy	a+a+a+{} newline {}{}{}{}{}a+a+a+a				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3143229				0	cy	Empty character strings are a simple way to ensure that texts and formulas are left-aligned. They are defined using double inverted commas "" . Make sure you do not use any typographic inverted commas. Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\align.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809				0	cy	"A further example." newline a+b newline ""c-d				2002-02-02 02:02:02
