helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	tit				0	cy	Basics				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	bm_id4488967				0	cy	\<bookmark_value\>Basic;fundamentals\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>subroutines\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>variables;global and local\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>modules;subroutines and functions\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154927				0	cy	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01010210.xhp\" name=\"Basics\"\>Basics\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156023				0	cy	This section provides the fundamentals for working with $[officename] Basic.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3147560				0	cy	$[officename] Basic code is based on subroutines and functions that are specified between \<emph\>sub...end sub\</emph\> and \<emph\>function...end function\</emph\> sections. Each Sub or Function can call other Subs and Functions. If you take care to write generic code for a Sub or Function, you can probably re-use it in other programs. See also \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020300.xhp\" name=\"Procedures and Functions\"\>Procedures and Functions\</link\>.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150398				0	cy	What is a Sub?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3148797				0	cy	\<emph\>Sub\</emph\> is the short form of \<emph\>subroutine\</emph\>, that is used to handle a certain task within a program. Subs are used to split a task into individual procedures. Splitting a program into procedures and sub-procedures enhances readability and reduces the error-proneness. A sub possibly takes some arguments as parameters but does not return any values back to the calling sub or function, for example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3150868				0	cy	DoSomethingWithTheValues(MyFirstValue,MySecondValue)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156282				0	cy	What is a Function?				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3156424				0	cy	A \<emph\>function\</emph\> is essentially a sub, which returns a value. You may use a function at the right side of a variable declaration, or at other places where you normally use values, for example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	cy	MySecondValue = myFunction(MyFirstValue)				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153364				0	cy	Global and local variables				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112				0	cy	Global variables are valid for all subs and functions inside a module. They are declared at the beginning of a module before the first sub or function starts.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3154012				0	cy	Variables that you declare within a sub or function are valid only inside this sub or function. These variables override global variables with the same name and local variables with the same name coming from superordinate subs or functions.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150010				0	cy	Structuring				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3153727				0	cy	After separating your program into procedures and functions (Subs and Functions), you can save these procedures and functions as files for reuse in other projects. $[officename] Basic supports \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020500.xhp\" name=\"Modules and Libraries\"\>Modules and Libraries\</link\>. Subs and functions are always contained in modules. You can define modules to be global or part of a document. Multiple modules can be combined to a library.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01010210.xhp	0	help	par_id3152578				0	cy	You can copy or move subs, functions, modules and libraries from one file to another by using the \<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01/06130000.xhp\" name=\"Macro\"\>Macro\</link\> dialog.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	tit				0	cy	InStr Function [Runtime]				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155934				0	cy	\<bookmark_value\>InStr function\</bookmark_value\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155934				0	cy	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120401.xhp\" name=\"InStr Function [Runtime]\"\>InStr Function [Runtime]\</link\>				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3153990				0	cy	Returns the position of a string within another string.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3147303				0	cy	The Instr function returns the position at which the match was found. If the string was not found, the function returns 0.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090				0	cy	Syntax:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3146957				0	cy	InStr ([Start As Long,] Text1 As String, Text2 As String[, Compare])				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148538				0	cy	Return value:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3149763				0	cy	Integer				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148473				0	cy	Parameters:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3153126	9			0	cy	\<emph\>Start: \</emph\>A numeric expression that marks the position in a string where the search for the specified substring starts. If you omit this parameter, the search starts at the first character of the string. The maximum allowed value is 65535.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3145609				0	cy	\<emph\>Text1:\</emph\> The string expression that you want to search.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3147559				0	cy	\<emph\>Text2:\</emph\> The string expression that you want to search for.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3154758				0	cy	\<emph\>Compare:\</emph\> Optional numeric expression that defines the type of comparison. The value of this parameter can be 0 or 1. The default value of 1 specifies a text comparison that is not case-sensitive. The value of 0 specifies a binary comparison that is case-sensitive.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3153361				0	cy	To avoid a run-time error, do not set the Compare parameter if the first return parameter is omitted.				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154366				0	cy	Example:				2002-02-02 02:02:02
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120401.xhp	0	help	par_id3154217				0	cy	Sub ExamplePosition				2002-02-02 02:02:02
